<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#concentration-camp">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Concentration camp</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A concentration camp is a place where large numbers of civilians or prisoners are detained, usually without trial, in harsh conditions. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> of 1899–1902, the British army interned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> women and children and black Africans in camps where many thousands died of disease. The camps damaged the international reputation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/concentration-camp.jpg" alt="Concentration camp" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Army Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boercamp1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The English term concentration camp comes from the reconcentration camps (Spanish: reconcentrados) set up by the Spanish military in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-years-war">Ten Years' War</a> of 1868–1878. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> used such camps again in the Cuban War of Independence of 1895–98, when Valeriano Weyler's Reconcentration policy moved rural Cubans into them. The label was later applied to camps run by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a>, by the British during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herero-and-nama-genocide">Herero and Nama genocide</a> of 1904–1907, in which the death rate in the camps was 45 per cent, twice that of the British camps. Civilian internment may be older still, since a first example may date to the 1830s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A concentration camp is a prison or other facility used to intern political prisoners or politically targeted groups, such as national or ethnic minorities, on grounds of national security, exploitation or punishment. The term covers systems that differ greatly in severity, mortality and architecture. What they share is that the inmates are held outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>. Extermination camps, whose main purpose is killing, are also loosely called concentration camps, and historians debate whether other cases of civilian internment should be called concentration camps or internment camps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, katorga was a punishment for the most serious crimes; about 6,000 convicts were serving it in 1906 and 28,600 in 1916. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> created separate prison camps under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>. In 1929 the camps were put under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogpu">Joint State Political Directorate</a> and expanded into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>, which held some 18 million people from 1929 until 1953.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>, deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> from mid-1915 were sent into camps across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> al-Ayn and Deir ez-Zor, where hunger, disease and killings followed. It is estimated that up to 700,000 Armenians were killed in these camps between 1915 and 1918.</p><p>Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> opened camps in 1933 for tens of thousands of political prisoners, mainly members of the Communist Party and the Social Democratic Party, then arrested so-called anti-socials from 1937 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> increasingly from 1938. About 1.65 million people were registered as prisoners, and about a million of them died.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Concentration camp” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Second Boer War (1899–1902) required some 450,000 imperial troops to defeat Boer forces that never numbered more than a fraction of that figure, and the deaths of thousands of Boer and African civilians in British <b>concentration camps</b> damaged Britain's international reputation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Terror was organised by the SS under Heinrich Himmler, which absorbed the police, ran the Gestapo and the <b>concentration camps</b> from Dachau, opened in March 1933, onwards, and in 1939 combined its security services in the Reich Security Main Office under Reinhard Heydrich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Steyn and de Wet refused to accept defeat and waged a guerrilla war for two more years, during which British forces burned farms across the Free State and moved much of its population into <b>concentration camps</b>, where mortality was very high.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1939 and 1943 army operations against the Mucubal killed hundreds and sent 3,529 prisoners to <b>concentration camps</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The state answered with protected villages, which the guerrillas compared to <b>concentration camps</b>, with raids into neighbouring states such as Operation Dingo in 1977, and with chemical and biological agents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The war of 1899–1902, in which the British Empire deployed some 450,000 troops against two small republics, was Britain’s most expensive conflict between the Napoleonic Wars and the First World War and provoked fierce criticism at home, above all over the <b>concentration camps</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
